turn on Fox News, you'll see appropriate pro-Putin&nbsp;
Apollonia. But for the most part, Putin's invasion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.200
of Ukraine has actually had the unintended&nbsp;
consequence of unifying and strengthening the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:34.320
West. There's an interesting article that you can&nbsp;
check out by Hollie Elliott on CNBC called Putin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.200
Always Wanted to Weaken the West. He's done the&nbsp;
exact opposite, and the article actually explains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.800
If there's anything we've learned about&nbsp;
Putin over the 22 years he's been in power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.000
it's that he is systematically and repeatedly&nbsp;
tried to weaken and undermine the West. But in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.840
his invasion of Ukraine, he seems to have achieved&nbsp;
the exact opposite, managing to unite most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.320
international community in its condemnation&nbsp;
of Russia's aggression towards its neighbor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.840
Ian Bremmer, president of political risk&nbsp;
consultancy Eurasia Group, commented quote Naito&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.040
is united more so than at any point since the&nbsp;
Soviet collapse, with a renewed sense of purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.840
and mission. So too is the European Union. Germany&nbsp;
supports ending their economic dependance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

